import json
from pathlib import Path
Q=[]
def q(d,ch,p,stem,options,correct,reasons):
 Q.append(dict(id=f'q{len(Q)+1:03}',domain=d,chapter=ch,page=p,stem=stem,options=options.split('~'),correct=correct,why=reasons.split('~')))
q(1,1,55,'A developer has AdministratorAccess in a member account. The parent OU denies EC2 launches outside approved Regions. What happens to an unapproved-Region launch?','It succeeds because AdministratorAccess is broader~It fails because the applicable explicit deny wins~It succeeds after enabling consolidated billing~It succeeds when the developer uses the member root user',[1],'AdministratorAccess cannot override an explicit deny.~SCP restrictions apply to this member-account request.~Billing does not change authorization.~Member-account root is also constrained by applicable SCPs.')
q(1,1,18,'A platform team lets developers create IAM roles but must prevent those roles from exceeding an approved permission scope. Which control best fits?','An IAM permissions boundary, enforced on created roles~An S3 lifecycle rule~A CloudWatch dashboard~A larger role session duration',[0],'A boundary limits the maximum effective identity permissions; enforce its attachment and prevent bypass.~Lifecycle controls object retention, not IAM delegation.~Visibility alone does not enforce the scope.~Session duration changes time, not permission scope.')
q(1,1,26,'A monitoring vendor serves many customers from one AWS account. A customer wants to prevent another customer from tricking the vendor into using its role. Choose TWO required design elements.','Trust the intended vendor principal~Require the vendor-assigned unique ExternalId~Publish a shared root password~Disable CloudTrail~Create a public S3 bucket',[0,1],'The role must trust the intended caller.~ExternalId ties the vendor request to this customer.~Root passwords are inappropriate and unrelated to the confused deputy control.~Disabling audit weakens visibility.~Public data access does not solve role assumption.')
q(1,1,65,'An enterprise wants employees to sign in once and receive different access levels across 40 AWS accounts, using its existing identity provider. What is the best starting point?','One shared IAM user in every account~Cognito identity pools for workforce administrators~IAM Identity Center with account permission-set assignments~A public API key for the management account',[2],'Shared users lose individual identity and increase credential management.~Cognito primarily targets application-user patterns; Identity Center fits workforce access.~It centralizes federation and per-account workforce permissions.~API keys do not authorize AWS administration.')
q(1,1,46,'A company must keep its AD users on premises and proxy supported AWS directory authentication to that directory. It accepts a dependency on its private connection. Which service fits?','Simple AD~AD Connector~A new untrusted directory in every account~A DynamoDB user table',[1],'Simple AD is a separate basic directory and lacks the required trust/proxy model.~AD Connector redirects authentication to the existing directory.~Duplicated directories violate the stated identity-management goal.~A custom user table is not an AD proxy.')
q(1,1,44,'A team establishes forest trust between on-premises AD and AWS Managed Microsoft AD. Which assumption is unsafe?','Trust can permit cross-domain authentication~Private network and DNS connectivity are necessary~Trust automatically copies all user objects to the AWS directory~The trust direction should match the access requirement',[2],'This is the purpose of a properly configured trust.~Authentication paths need network/DNS support.~Trust and directory replication are different mechanisms.~Direction is a deliberate authorization design choice.')
q(1,1,74,'A networking team owns subnets; application accounts in the same organization should deploy and own their own resources inside those subnets. What fits?','Consolidated billing only~Share the subnets through AWS RAM~Copy the networking root credentials~Make all subnets publicly accessible',[1],'Billing does not share subnet resources.~VPC subnet sharing provides the requested ownership split.~Root credential sharing is unnecessary and unsafe.~Public access is unrelated to cross-account resource ownership.')
q(1,1,70,'A business is establishing a governed multi-account AWS environment with baseline logging and repeatable account provisioning. Which choice minimizes setup effort?','Manual account creation and emailed checklists~An EC2 instance with a spreadsheet~AWS Control Tower and Account Factory~CloudFront signed cookies',[2],'Manual steps create unnecessary effort and inconsistency.~This is not a governance/provisioning service.~Control Tower and Account Factory target this requirement.~Signed cookies authorize CDN content access.')
q(1,1,59,'A policy uses ForAllValues:StringEquals on aws:TagKeys with Env and CostCenter. Why is that alone insufficient to require both tags?','It automatically creates missing tags~It only checks the supplied keys against the permitted set~It encrypts the tag values~It disables resource tagging',[1],'Policies do not create tags as a side effect.~A subset or missing context can satisfy the set test; enforce required presence separately.~Tag matching is not encryption.~The operator evaluates a condition rather than disabling tagging.')
q(1,2,89,'A security team needs durable audit logs from organization accounts in an isolated account. Which combination is most suitable?','CloudWatch CPU metrics and local instance disks~An organization trail and a protected central S3 destination~VPC peering without logging configuration~Public S3 access logs copied manually',[1],'CPU metrics do not record API activity and local disks lack suitable isolation.~A trail with correct policies and protected retention supports central auditing.~Connectivity does not create an audit trail.~Manual public storage is unnecessary and weakens protection.')
q(1,2,108,'Account B can read a secret resource policy in account A, but GetSecretValue for a KMS-encrypted secret fails. Which additional permission path should be checked?','Route 53 geolocation~CloudFront cache TTL~KMS key policy and caller decrypt permissions~EC2 placement group',[2],'DNS geography is unrelated to decryption authorization.~Cache TTL does not grant secret access.~Cross-account encrypted-secret access needs the appropriate KMS authorization as well.~Placement does not affect key permissions.')
q(1,3,295,'On-premises DNS must resolve private names hosted in an AWS private hosted zone over an existing VPN. What is needed?','A Resolver inbound endpoint and on-premises forwarding rules~A Resolver outbound endpoint only~A public S3 website~A NAT Gateway in the on-premises network',[0],'Inbound endpoints accept DNS queries coming into AWS; configure routing and DNS controls.~Outbound endpoints send AWS-originating queries out to another resolver.~Static hosting does not resolve private DNS.~NAT alone does not implement the required DNS forwarding.')
q(1,3,298,'Several VPCs need to resolve corp.internal through the same corporate DNS servers. What design reduces duplicated forwarding management?','Duplicate public records for every private service~Share supported outbound Resolver rules through RAM~Use CNAME records at every zone apex~Disable VPC DNS support',[1],'Public duplication is unnecessary and may expose internal naming.~Shared Resolver rules centralize the forwarding configuration.~CNAME at a zone apex is not valid DNS design.~Disabling DNS support breaks the intended resolver behavior.')
q(1,13,675,'Production and development VPCs attach to a Transit Gateway. Both need shared services, but they must not communicate with each other. What should be designed?','One default route to every attachment~Separate TGW routing tables with appropriate associations and propagation~A larger Transit Gateway name~VPC peering between all VPCs',[1],'Unrestricted routes contradict segmentation.~Routing tables can expose shared services while excluding prohibited paths.~Names do not enforce routing.~Full mesh peering creates unwanted paths and extra management.')
q(1,13,687,'A SaaS provider must privately expose one TCP service to many customer VPCs, including customers with overlapping CIDRs. It does not need full network connectivity. What fits?','Full-mesh VPC peering~A provider NLB and customer PrivateLink interface endpoints~A public Internet Gateway route in every customer subnet~Sharing all provider route tables',[1],'Peering conflicts with overlapping CIDRs and broadens connectivity unnecessarily.~PrivateLink exposes the service without full network peering.~The requirement is private service access.~Route sharing is not the required supported service-exposure mechanism.')
q(1,13,704,'A company uses Direct Connect to reach a Transit Gateway through a Direct Connect gateway. Which VIF type is appropriate?','Public VIF~Private VIF~Transit VIF~No VIF is needed',[2],'Public VIF targets supported public AWS services.~Private VIF fits other VPC/VGW connectivity patterns.~Transit VIF is the relevant DX gateway-to-TGW path.~DX logical service connectivity requires the appropriate virtual interface.')
q(1,13,712,'A critical hybrid application must survive the loss of a Direct Connect facility. Which option best addresses that specific failure?','Two cables in one LAG at the same facility~A larger single connection~Connections through multiple independent DX locations~A longer DNS TTL',[2],'One facility remains a common failure point.~Bandwidth does not add location resilience.~Location diversity directly addresses facility loss.~DNS caching does not supply a surviving physical path.')
q(1,1,16,'An application role in account A reads a table in A and writes objects to a bucket in B without switching roles. Which permissions are generally needed? Choose TWO.','Caller-role permissions for the required actions~A suitable bucket policy in B authorizing cross-account writes~A member account in two organizations simultaneously~A new root access key in B~A public bucket ACL',[0,1],'The caller must have permission for its operations.~The destination resource needs the relevant cross-account authorization.~Organization membership does not provide these permissions.~Root access keys are unnecessary.~Public access is unnecessary for scoped cross-account access.')
q(1,10,564,'Every new account in an OU must receive the same audit IAM role and baseline resources. Which deployment tool best fits?','CloudFormation StackSets with organization automatic deployment~One CloudFormation stack in a developer laptop~A manual runbook copied to email~An S3 signed URL',[0],'StackSets deploy stack instances across accounts/Regions and support automatic account onboarding.~A single stack does not span all target accounts by itself.~Manual repetition defeats the requirement.~Signed URLs authorize object access, not organization-wide provisioning.')
q(1,1,62,'An organization wants consistent tag spelling and allowed values, plus prevention of missing mandatory tags where supported. What is the strongest design?','Tag policies alone guarantee every resource has all tags~Tag policies plus appropriate request-tag enforcement and compliance checks~Cost Explorer alone~A single user naming convention',[1],'Tag policies do not universally prevent untagged creation.~Use complementary controls for consistency, presence and detection.~Reporting does not enforce tags.~A user naming rule does not control resource tags.')
q(2,3,239,'A web platform routes /orders and /search to different HTTP services and uses WebSockets. Which load balancer best fits?','Application Load Balancer~Network Load Balancer alone~Gateway Load Balancer~No load balancer can route HTTP paths',[0],'ALB supports HTTP-aware routing and WebSockets.~NLB does not inspect HTTP paths.~GWLB targets network inspection appliances.~HTTP path routing is a core ALB capability.')
q(2,3,242,'A service uses UDP and customers allow-list fixed regional endpoint IPs. Which front end is the best fit?','API Gateway REST API~Application Load Balancer~Network Load Balancer with appropriate static addresses~S3 Transfer Acceleration',[2],'REST API Gateway is not a generic UDP front end.~ALB handles HTTP-family traffic rather than UDP.~NLB supports UDP and static zonal addresses.~S3 acceleration transfers objects, not generic UDP traffic.')
q(2,3,210,'A team has a containerized HTTP application and wants no EC2 host patching or host-capacity management. It has no Kubernetes requirement. What fits best?','ECS on Fargate~Self-managed Kubernetes on EC2~Dedicated Hosts with manual Docker installation~One large persistent EC2 instance',[0],'Fargate removes EC2 host management for supported ECS tasks.~It adds unnecessary control-plane/node operations.~It increases host administration.~It retains host management and creates a singleton risk.')
q(2,3,207,'An ECS application must write invoices to S3 while each task receives only its own necessary permissions. Where should these application permissions be placed?','Task IAM role~Image tag~ALB listener~CloudWatch log retention policy',[0],'The task role supplies AWS permissions to application code.~An image tag is a version label, not an identity policy.~Listener rules route traffic rather than grant S3 access.~Log retention controls storage duration.')
q(2,3,233,'A Lambda function must call an external API that accepts requests only from a fixed public IP. What architecture fits?','Attach Lambda to a public subnet and assume it gets a public IP~Use VPC private subnets with a NAT Gateway/EIP egress path~Increase Lambda memory~Use a security group with an outbound source-IP rule',[1],'VPC-attached Lambda does not receive a public IP merely from subnet placement.~The NAT EIP supplies the consistent outbound address; configure routes and SGs.~Memory does not stabilize source addresses.~SGs filter allowed traffic, not translate its source address.')
q(2,3,256,'Clients upload 120 MB video files. API Gateway authenticates the user, but proxying the full file exceeds its payload limit. What is the best design?','Split every upload into thousands of API calls~Return a scoped presigned S3 upload URL and upload directly~Raise Lambda concurrency only~Cache the upload response in CloudFront',[1],'This adds complexity when direct object upload fits.~The API authorizes the operation while bytes bypass the gateway payload ceiling.~Concurrency does not change payload size limits.~Response caching does not solve upload transfer size.')
q(2,3,267,'A mobile app needs one GraphQL endpoint that combines DynamoDB data with a custom HTTP source and supports live updates. Which service fits?','AWS AppSync~AWS Batch~AWS Backup~AWS Directory Service',[0],'AppSync provides managed GraphQL resolvers and real-time patterns.~Batch schedules finite compute jobs.~Backup protects recovery data.~Directory Service manages compatible directory capabilities.')
q(2,3,277,'A global web application should direct DNS queries toward the AWS Region with the lowest measured latency, not simply the same country as the user. Which routing policy fits?','Geolocation~Latency-based~Simple without health checks~IP-based with no CIDR mapping',[1],'Geolocation implements location matching rather than latency selection.~Latency-based routing matches the stated objective.~Simple routing does not compare regional latency.~IP-based routing needs explicit mappings and serves a different goal.')
q(2,3,299,'A global TCP application needs stable anycast IPs and health-based routing across regional endpoints. It does not need object caching. Which service fits?','CloudFront alone~Global Accelerator~S3 replication alone~Route 53 private hosted zone',[1],'CloudFront is an HTTP content-delivery service and not a generic TCP accelerator.~Global Accelerator fits stable global IP and supported TCP/UDP endpoint routing.~Replication moves objects rather than routes TCP sessions.~Private DNS does not supply global anycast application ingress.')
q(2,4,328,'Linux application servers in three AZs need concurrent access to the same managed filesystem. Which option is appropriate?','One EBS volume with ordinary ext4 attached across AZs~Regional EFS with mount targets and permissions~Each server’s instance store~S3 as a fully POSIX-compatible disk',[1],'EBS is AZ-scoped and ordinary filesystems are not safe shared multi-writer designs.~EFS provides shared NFS semantics across AZs.~Local disks do not provide a shared durable filesystem.~S3 objects are not a fully POSIX-compatible shared disk.')
q(2,4,356,'A Windows application requires SMB shares, NTFS permissions and Active Directory integration. Which storage service best fits?','Amazon EFS~FSx for Windows File Server~Instance store only~DynamoDB',[1],'EFS targets NFS/POSIX rather than Windows SMB semantics.~FSx Windows directly supports the required file-sharing model.~Local ephemeral storage is not a managed shared SMB service.~A key-value database does not provide SMB shares.')
q(2,4,357,'An HPC team processes S3 datasets with a parallel filesystem and wants to start before the entire dataset is downloaded. Which option fits?','FSx for Lustre with S3 integration and lazy loading~S3 Glacier Deep Archive as a live mounted disk~A single small EBS boot volume~An SNS topic',[0],'Lustre supplies parallel filesystem access and supported lazy-loading behavior.~Deep Archive requires restoration and is not a live POSIX disk.~A small single disk does not meet the shared HPC access requirement.~SNS is messaging, not a filesystem.')
q(2,2,134,'A company must prevent deletion of protected S3 object versions during a fixed regulatory retention period, even by privileged users. Which mode fits?','Object Lock compliance mode with the required retention~Governance mode with bypass permission for everyone~An object tag named immutable~A CloudWatch alarm after deletion',[0],'Compliance retention protects the version from early deletion/shortening under its rules.~Bypass permissions weaken the required protection.~A label alone does not enforce retention.~An after-the-fact alarm does not prevent deletion.')
q(2,5,388,'A paid course streams many protected files through CloudFront. The application should authorize access without rewriting every segment URL. What fits?','CloudFront signed cookies and a protected origin~A public S3 ACL on every video~S3 lifecycle expiration after one hour~An IAM username inside the filename',[0],'Signed cookies can authorize access to a protected file set.~Public access bypasses paid authorization.~Lifecycle deletes data; it does not grant a temporary viewer session.~Filenames are not access-control credentials.')
q(2,5,398,'A CDN needs a lightweight JavaScript URL rewrite before cache lookup, with no external network calls. Which edge compute choice best fits?','A continuously running EC2 fleet~CloudFront Functions viewer-request function~A Batch multi-node job~A Lambda function behind an SQS queue',[1],'This adds unnecessary infrastructure for a small edge task.~It fits lightweight viewer-request customization.~Batch is unrelated to synchronous edge rewriting.~Queue processing does not rewrite an in-flight viewer request.')
q(2,6,413,'A DynamoDB table needs a new query path using a different partition key after the table already exists. What should be considered?','A Local Secondary Index added now~A Global Secondary Index~A longer TTL~A larger S3 bucket',[1],'LSIs must be defined at table creation and retain the partition key.~GSIs provide alternate keys and can be added later.~TTL controls expiry, not alternate key lookup.~S3 capacity is unrelated to DynamoDB query keys.')
q(2,6,430,'A bursty Lambda application creates more database connections than RDS can handle. Queries are otherwise efficient. What should be considered first?','RDS Proxy with suitable networking/authentication~S3 Transfer Acceleration~A higher DNS TTL only~CloudFront geo restriction',[0],'Connection pooling addresses the identified connection storm.~This accelerates S3 transfers, not DB connections.~Caching DNS does not pool backend connections.~Country restriction does not solve the connection architecture.')
q(2,6,435,'An Aurora application has heavy analytical reads and ordinary transactional reads. It wants analytical connections to use only two larger replicas. What fits?','Use the writer endpoint for everything~Create a custom endpoint for the selected replicas~Change every SQL query to a write~Use S3 signed URLs',[1],'This can increase writer contention and ignores replica separation.~Custom endpoints target a selected subset of instances.~Writes are not a substitute for analytical reads.~Object URLs do not route database connections.')
q(2,7,446,'An approval workflow can wait several days and must retain execution history. Which orchestration option best fits?','One Lambda sleeping until approval~Step Functions Standard with a callback/wait design~Step Functions Express for an unlimited duration~A browser tab kept open',[1],'A standard Lambda invocation cannot run for days.~Standard workflows support long-lived orchestration and suitable callback patterns.~Express has a much shorter maximum duration.~A browser session is not a durable workflow engine.')
q(2,7,469,'Each order must independently reach shipping, fraud and accounting. A slow fraud consumer must not block the others. What is the best design?','One SQS queue with three competing consumer types~SNS fan-out to one SQS queue per consumer type~Three consumers reading the same local file~A shared instance-memory list',[1],'Competing consumers do not each receive every message.~Separate queues give independent processing/backlogs and fan-out delivery.~A local file creates coupling and weak durability.~Instance memory does not provide the required durable distribution.')
q(2,8,486,'An event stream must support two independent consumers and replay of earlier events for several days. Which service is the clearest fit?','Amazon Data Firehose alone~Kinesis Data Streams with suitable retention~SNS email subscriptions~A non-persistent local buffer',[1],'Firehose is delivery-focused, not a general replay log.~Data Streams supports retained events and independent consumers.~Email is not the required replayable stream.~A local buffer lacks the required durable service behavior.')
q(2,8,521,'A team runs occasional SQL analysis on partitioned data in S3 and wants minimal infrastructure management. Which service is the best starting point?','Athena~A permanently running manually managed database cluster~One EC2 instance per CSV file~Amazon MQ',[0],'Athena is a managed serverless SQL query option for S3.~This adds unnecessary standing administration for occasional queries.~Per-file VMs are inefficient and unnecessary.~MQ is a broker, not an S3 SQL query service.')
q(3,5,404,'A product API repeatedly reads the same records and tolerates 30 seconds of staleness. The database read load is high. Which improvement directly addresses this?','Add a cache with an explicit TTL/invalidation strategy~Increase log retention to ten years~Use synchronous cross-Region writes for every read~Disable all monitoring',[0],'A suitable cache reduces repeated reads within the accepted staleness window.~Retention does not reduce database requests.~This increases complexity and does not cache reads.~Removing visibility does not remove the bottleneck.')
q(3,3,195,'An ASG launch template now points to a patched AMI. Existing instances still run the old image. What should be done?','Wait for the template name to propagate~Perform a controlled Instance Refresh~Increase CloudWatch log retention~Rename the Auto Scaling group',[1],'Template changes do not modify running instance images.~Instance Refresh replaces existing instances using the desired configuration.~Log retention does not patch instances.~Names have no effect on instance software.')
q(3,3,193,'A queue-backed worker fleet processes jobs of varying arrival rate. CPU is poorly correlated with demand. Which scaling signal is usually more useful?','Total account bill~Queue backlog per worker, calibrated to processing time~Number of IAM users~S3 bucket creation date',[1],'Billing is delayed and unrelated to immediate queue demand.~Backlog per worker connects capacity to queued work and latency goals.~Identity count does not track workload demand.~Bucket age does not indicate worker saturation.')
q(3,2,159,'Security needs a history of resource configurations and detection when a security group permits unrestricted SSH. Which service fits?','AWS Config~AWS Batch~Amazon Polly~S3 Transfer Acceleration',[0],'Config records configuration state and evaluates compliance rules.~Batch executes jobs.~Polly synthesizes speech.~Transfer Acceleration improves supported object transfer paths.')
q(3,2,163,'An instance exhibits suspicious network/API behavior consistent with compromise. Which service is designed to surface such threat findings from supported telemetry?','GuardDuty~CodeBuild~Data Lifecycle Manager~Service Catalog',[0],'GuardDuty detects suspicious activity using supported signals.~Build automation is not threat detection.~DLM manages EBS snapshot/AMI lifecycle.~A product catalog does not analyze compromise telemetry.')
q(3,2,157,'An engineering team wants continuous vulnerability assessment for supported container images in ECR. Which integration fits?','Enhanced image scanning with Amazon Inspector~CloudTrail data retention only~A DynamoDB TTL~A Route 53 Alias',[0],'Inspector-backed enhanced scanning targets relevant image vulnerabilities.~API logs do not assess package vulnerabilities by themselves.~TTL removes items rather than scans images.~DNS aliases do not inspect software.')
q(3,9,537,'A security analytics pipeline must process new CloudWatch log events promptly. An existing S3 export workflow is too delayed. What should replace the live ingestion path?','A longer export interval~A CloudWatch Logs subscription to a supported destination~A manual monthly download~Disabling detailed logs',[1],'Longer intervals increase latency.~Subscriptions provide a streaming path for new matching events.~Monthly manual export violates prompt processing.~Less telemetry does not meet the analytics requirement.')
q(3,9,546,'An API has high end-to-end latency, but average EC2 CPU is low. Engineers need to locate the slow downstream call within individual requests. What is most helpful?','Distributed tracing with suitable instrumentation~A larger CPU limit without investigation~A different account name~Disabling the ALB access logs',[0],'Traces connect a request to time spent in downstream segments.~The evidence does not identify CPU as the bottleneck.~Account naming is irrelevant.~Removing logs reduces diagnostic evidence.')
q(3,3,247,'Two AZs receive equal shares of 100 requests/s. AZ A has two targets and B has eight. With cross-zone balancing disabled, what is the approximate per-target load under uniform within-AZ distribution?','A: 10/s; B: 10/s~A: 25/s; B: 6.25/s~A: 50/s; B: 50/s~A: 2/s; B: 8/s',[1],'That is the uniform cross-zone result, not the stated design.~Each AZ receives 50/s: divide by two or eight targets.~Each target does not receive the whole AZ share.~Target counts are not request rates.')
q(3,6,417,'A DynamoDB workload repeatedly reads the same items with eventual consistency and needs lower read latency. Writes are not the bottleneck. What fits?','DAX with supported client integration~DAX to cache all strongly consistent reads~Moving all keys into one hot partition~Increasing the item size beyond the service limit',[0],'DAX accelerates supported cached reads.~Strongly consistent reads are not served from the DAX cache.~Concentrating load worsens distribution.~Item size limits still apply and larger items increase cost.')
q(3,11,608,'Compute Optimizer recommends instance rightsizing, but the workload has important memory peaks that are not visible. What should the team do before accepting the recommendation?','Assume CPU fully predicts memory~Collect memory telemetry and validate peak requirements~Delete all historical metrics~Always choose the smallest instance',[1],'CPU and memory are different resource constraints.~Memory evidence plus workload validation reduces unsafe downsizing.~Deleting evidence reduces confidence.~Smallest does not mean adequate or cheapest overall.')
q(3,11,602,'A team wants to reduce unintentional development overspending. Choose TWO accurate statements about AWS Budgets.','It can send threshold notifications~Configured Budget Actions can perform supported responses~It guarantees an exact real-time hard cap on all spending~A notification automatically terminates every resource~It makes cost-data latency disappear',[0,1],'Notifications are a core capability.~Supported actions need explicit configuration/permissions.~Billing latency and ongoing usage prevent this guarantee.~Notification alone does not implement termination.~Budgeting does not remove data-update delays.')
q(3,11,591,'A company has a stable compute baseline but expects to shift between EC2 families, Regions and Fargate. Which commitment is generally the most flexible fit for eligible usage?','An EC2 Instance Savings Plan fixed to one family/Region~A Compute Savings Plan sized to the baseline~A commitment sized to the largest one-time peak~Only Dedicated Hosts',[1],'Its flexibility is narrower than required.~Compute Savings Plans match broad eligible compute flexibility.~Overcommitting to a peak risks paying for unused commitment.~Dedicated tenancy is not required and does not provide this same flexibility.')
q(3,8,520,'Athena scan costs are high for daily queries that use a few columns from one date. Choose TWO improvements.','Convert suitable data to compressed Parquet~Partition data by useful query predicates such as date~Put every record in a separate tiny file~Remove date filters~Query every column every time',[0,1],'Columnar compression reduces unnecessary bytes read.~Partition pruning can avoid scanning unrelated dates.~Tiny files increase planning/request overhead.~Removing filters increases scanned data.~Selecting unnecessary columns undermines columnar savings.')
q(3,10,559,'A Lambda release should expose only 10% of traffic initially and roll back if a configured error alarm fires. What fits?','Overwrite production code with no version~CodeDeploy with Lambda aliases, canary traffic shift and alarm rollback~Change the S3 storage class~Create another IAM group',[1],'Unversioned overwrite removes controlled traffic shifting and weakens rollback.~These mechanisms support staged release and monitored reversal.~Storage class does not route Lambda version traffic.~IAM grouping is not a deployment strategy.')
q(3,10,565,'An operator manually changes an infrastructure property outside CloudFormation. The team wants to identify supported differences from the template. What should it use?','CloudFormation drift detection~A longer stack name~CloudFront invalidation~SQS visibility timeout',[0],'Drift detection compares supported actual state with expected template properties.~Names do not compare configuration.~Cache invalidation does not inspect infrastructure templates.~Visibility controls queue processing, not infrastructure state.')
q(3,13,719,'A NAT interface flow record shows ACCEPT for traffic from a public address. Does this prove an unsolicited connection reached a private EC2 instance?','Yes, ACCEPT proves every hop and application accepted it~No; inspect the interface and full path because NAT can still drop unsolicited traffic~Yes, NAT forwards all inbound traffic~No, because Flow Logs never record IP addresses',[1],'The record is scoped to a point in the path, not end-to-end success.~NAT behavior and downstream records must be considered.~NAT does not permit arbitrary unsolicited inbound sessions.~Flow Logs do record address/port metadata.')
q(3,7,456,'An application scales rapidly and overwhelms a partner API limited to a fixed processing rate. Choose TWO architectural improvements.','Buffer requests durably in SQS~Control consumer concurrency/rate to match the partner~Invoke unlimited asynchronous Lambda functions~Only increase producer instance count~Delete failed requests immediately',[0,1],'The queue absorbs bursts and supports retryable work.~The downstream dependency needs an explicit processing limit.~Unlimited asynchronous scaling can preserve the overload.~More producers can worsen the bottleneck.~Discarding failures violates reliable processing.')
q(4,12,615,'A company moves its self-managed PostgreSQL database to RDS PostgreSQL with only small application changes, to reduce database operations. Which migration strategy best describes this?','Retire~Replatform~Repurchase~Refactor into unrelated microservices',[1],'The database remains in use.~The platform improves while core architecture/engine largely remains.~The company is not replacing the application with another product.~A full architectural redesign is not described.')
q(4,12,649,'A company must move a fleet of supported physical and virtual servers to AWS with minimal redesign and a planned cutover. Which service best fits?','Application Migration Service (MGN)~Amazon Personalize~AWS Config only~CloudFront Functions',[0],'MGN is designed for server rehosting with replication/cutover.~Personalize builds recommendation workflows.~Configuration recording alone does not migrate servers.~An edge function cannot replicate server disks.')
q(4,12,650,'A business will retain its data center but needs ongoing block-level replication and tested recovery of supported servers into AWS after a disaster. Which service fits?','DMS for every disk~Elastic Disaster Recovery (DRS)~CodeBuild~Amazon SES',[1],'DMS migrates supported database data, not general server disks.~DRS targets server recovery readiness and failover/failback.~Build automation does not provide disk-replication DR.~Email delivery is unrelated.')
q(4,12,633,'An Oracle database will move to an Aurora PostgreSQL-compatible design with minimal downtime. Choose TWO key workstreams.','Assess and convert schema/application incompatibilities~Use a supported full-load plus CDC migration and validate cutover~Assume identical stored procedure behavior~Only copy the DNS record~Turn off all source logging before CDC',[0,1],'Different engines need compatibility and schema/application work.~Full load plus CDC can reduce cutover downtime when configured and validated.~Stored procedures and features can differ significantly.~DNS does not move schema/data.~CDC depends on supported source change information.')
q(4,12,631,'A full-load-plus-CDC migration has finished loading tables, but replication lag is still significant. What is the best cutover approach?','Immediately redirect writes and ignore lag~Validate data, coordinate a write pause and let required changes catch up before redirecting~Delete the source first~Only increase the destination DNS TTL',[1],'Unapplied changes can produce missing or inconsistent data.~A controlled catch-up and validation process protects cutover consistency.~Deleting the source destroys a rollback/reference path.~Longer DNS caching can complicate redirection without fixing lag.')
q(4,4,364,'A team must migrate a large NFS dataset to EFS over an available network, preserving supported metadata and validating transfers. What is the best starting point?','AWS DataSync~Amazon SES~A CloudWatch alarm alone~Manual screenshots of each directory',[0],'DataSync directly targets supported file transfer/synchronization and verification.~Email is not a file migration service.~An alarm does not move files.~Screenshots do not preserve files or metadata.')
q(4,4,371,'External partners must keep using SFTP, while files should land in S3 without the company managing SFTP servers. Which service fits?','Transfer Family~DynamoDB Streams~CloudFormation drift detection~CloudHSM alone',[0],'Transfer Family provides managed supported file-transfer endpoints.~Streams captures database changes, not SFTP sessions.~Drift detects configuration differences.~Cryptographic hardware does not supply an SFTP endpoint by itself.')
q(4,12,618,'A branch application requires NFS access to files backed by S3 and low-latency access to recent files. Which gateway fits?','Tape Gateway~S3 File Gateway~Stored Volume Gateway only~An Internet Gateway',[1],'Tape Gateway presents a virtual tape interface.~File Gateway supplies NFS/SMB access with caching.~Volume Gateway exposes iSCSI blocks rather than NFS shares.~An IGW provides network connectivity, not file semantics.')
q(4,12,619,'A company requires its complete iSCSI volume dataset to remain on premises, with backups in AWS. Which Volume Gateway mode fits?','Cached volumes~Stored volumes~CloudFront cache mode~S3 Intelligent-Tiering',[1],'Cached mode places the primary full data in AWS with local hot data.~Stored mode retains the full local dataset with cloud backup.~CDN caching is unrelated to iSCSI volume modes.~Object tiering does not provide the required local volume architecture.')
q(4,12,638,'A service may lose no more than 15 minutes of recent data and must be restored within one hour. What are the objectives?','RTO 15 minutes; RPO one hour~RPO 15 minutes; RTO one hour~Both describe availability percentage~Both require active-active by definition',[1],'This reverses the definitions.~RPO concerns recoverable data age; RTO concerns service restoration time.~These are recovery objectives, not uptime percentages.~The objectives do not prescribe a single architecture without further analysis.')
q(4,12,642,'A recovery environment already runs the complete application at reduced capacity and scales up during failover. Which DR strategy is this?','Backup and restore~Pilot light with only the database ready~Warm standby~Retire',[2],'The described application is already running, unlike a restore-only environment.~The complete stack is running, not just essential core components.~Warm standby is a scaled-down working recovery environment.~Retiring stops using a workload rather than prepares recovery.')
q(4,12,656,'Recovery copies must be centrally scheduled across supported AWS services and protected against premature deletion. Choose TWO relevant features.','AWS Backup plans and resource assignments~Appropriately configured Backup Vault Lock retention controls~A public bucket ACL~One untested snapshot on the source machine~Disabling encryption keys immediately',[0,1],'Plans/assignments organize supported backup coverage.~Vault Lock can protect retention according to its mode and lock process.~Public access does not enforce backup immutability.~A local untested copy does not meet centralized protection.~Destroying key access can destroy recoverability.')
q(4,7,462,'A legacy messaging application depends on supported ActiveMQ protocols. The company wants to migrate with minimal application changes and reduce broker management. Which choice fits?','Rewrite immediately to an unrelated protocol~Amazon MQ with validated engine compatibility and HA~Use S3 as an identical broker~Use CloudFront as the queue',[1],'A full rewrite conflicts with minimal changes.~Amazon MQ fits supported broker/protocol migration.~Object storage does not reproduce broker semantics.~A CDN is not a message broker.')
q(4,3,182,'A modernization plan proposes moving every x86 instance to Graviton. Some workloads run Windows. What should the architect conclude?','All operating systems run unchanged on Arm~Validate OS/binary compatibility; do not move the Windows workloads blindly~Only change the instance tag~Convert storage to Glacier to fix CPU compatibility',[1],'CPU architecture and OS support matter.~Compatibility is a hard migration constraint.~Tags do not transform executables or operating systems.~Storage tiering cannot change CPU compatibility.')
q(4,12,647,'A migration program lacks a reliable view of server dependencies and utilization. What should happen before defining migration waves?','Move servers in alphabetical order~Collect discovery/dependency data and build an assessed migration plan~Choose the most expensive instance for every server~Remove all existing monitoring',[1],'Alphabetical order ignores application dependencies.~Discovery evidence supports suitable grouping, sizing and sequencing.~Uniform oversizing does not solve dependency planning.~Removing monitoring makes assessment harder.')
assert len(Q)==75,len(Q)
for x in Q:
 assert len(x['options'])==len(x['why'])
 assert all(0<=a<len(x['options']) for a in x['correct'])
(Path(__file__).resolve().parent/'dist/questions.json').write_text(json.dumps(Q,ensure_ascii=False))
print('75 questions; all options have explanations')
